============================================================================
(1) THE TWO CONDITIONS ARE ONE: b DEPENDS ONLY ON N/v
============================================================================
       N      v     N/v |            b            k
  --------------------------------------------------
       2   1.00   2.000 |   0.01393690   1.36461229
       4   2.00   2.000 |   0.01393690   1.36461229
       1   0.50   2.000 |   0.01393690   1.36461229
       6   3.00   2.000 |   0.01393690   1.36461229
       3   1.50   2.000 |   0.01393690   1.36461229

  Every row is the booked point. So 'exactly two species doped' and 'v = 1' are not
  two facts about the host -- they are one relation, N = 2v, and supplying either
  alone reduces the debt by nothing. Section 6c counts them separately (three unmet,
  one supplied); on its own formulas the screening pair is a single condition.

============================================================================
(2) THE FENCE ON N/v
============================================================================
  anchor within a factor 2    N/v in [1.8245, 2.1845]   = 2 -8.8% / +9.2%
  anchor within a factor 10   N/v in [1.4516, 2.6472]   = 2 -27.4% / +32.4%
  anchor within a factor 100  N/v in [1.0024, 3.3910]   = 2 -49.9% / +69.6%

  So the ratio is fenced at roughly +-9% for factor-2 accuracy on the anchor. Among
  INTEGER species counts at v = 1 there is no room at all:

     N |          k |   anchor vs booked
  ----------------------------------------
     1 |    1.58305 |               x101
     2 |    1.36461 |                 x1
     3 |    1.23773 |            x0.0323
     4 |    1.14832 |           x0.00183
     6 |    1.02355 |          x1.43e-05

  N = 1 overshoots by two orders and N = 3 undershoots by one and a half, so the
  count is not merely favoured, it is isolated. The tolerance the fence allows is
  continuous room, and the only continuous variable in the ratio is v.

============================================================================
(3) WHAT THIS DOES TO THE DOCKET
============================================================================
  Hold N = 2 and the fence reads v in [0.9155, 1.0962] for factor-2 accuracy,
  i.e. the band velocity must sit within 8.4% of the medium's gauge-field speed.
  Section 6c argues that comes free -- a linear node IS a cone whose slope defines the
  emergent light speed -- and on the degeneracy above that argument is doing more work
  than it was credited with: it is what stops N and v trading off against each other.

  So the residue is one number and not two. If the cone argument holds, v = 1 is fixed
  and the whole of #146 is 'why exactly one node pair is doped'. If it ever weakens,
  N and v move together exactly, and a host with N = 4 would need v = 2 -- fermions
  outrunning the medium's own gauge field, which is its own refutation. That is worth
  recording: the degeneracy is not a loophole, because its far end is unphysical.
